### Guard every key-list assignment with the equality check

**Severity:** `high`

Once `Keys` is non-null, the left side of this `||` is always true, so every subsequent assignment still calls `SetValue` and recreates the bound key visuals. That defeats the duplicate-update guard on the normal non-null path and can leave the reported held-key flicker unfixed. Let the helper handle all null and non-null combinations before updating the dependency property.

```suggestion
            set
            {
                if (!JudgeIfKeyValueSame(value))
                {
                    SetValue(KeysProperty, value);
                }
            }
```

### Compare key visuals in display order

**Severity:** `medium`

`HashSet.SetEquals` ignores both ordering and duplicate multiplicity, but `Keys` is rendered as an ordered list. A reordered shortcut can therefore be treated as unchanged and leave stale keycaps on screen. Compare corresponding entries instead; this preserves display order and avoids allocating a set on repeated key-down events.

```suggestion
            for (int index = 0; index < currentValue.Count; index++)
            {
                if (!Equals(currentValue[index], newValue[index]))
                {
                    return false;
                }
            }

            return true;
```
